# Evolving Nested Domain Contracts Safely

Nested writes are a consumer contract, not just a server implementation detail.
This workbook publishes projection-specific snapshots and classifies changes to
relationship modes and bounds before a runtime consumes them.

## Setup

```elixir
Code.require_file(Path.expand("support/bootstrap.exs", __DIR__))
SelectoLivebooksNotebookBootstrap.install_verification!()
```

## 1) Compile a stable consumer release

```elixir
import ExUnit.Assertions
alias Selecto.Domain.ConsumerProjectionRelease

domain = Selecto.Domain.CompositionFixtures.order_document()
assert {:ok, previous} = ConsumerProjectionRelease.compile(domain,
  projection_id: "order-editor", consumer: "warehouse-ui", version: "1.0.0")
assert previous["composition"]["relationships"]["items"]["write"]["max_items"] == 200
assert is_binary(previous["fingerprint"])

assert {:ok, same_again} = ConsumerProjectionRelease.compile(domain,
  projection_id: "order-editor", consumer: "warehouse-ui", version: "1.0.0")
assert same_again["fingerprint"] == previous["fingerprint"]
```

## 2) Recognize a compatible expansion

Raising an upper bound accepts everything the old consumer could submit, so the
relationship diff classifies it as compatible.

```elixir
expanded = put_in(domain, [:writes, :relationships, "items", :write, :max_items], 250)
assert {:ok, expanded_release} = ConsumerProjectionRelease.compile(expanded,
  projection_id: "order-editor", consumer: "warehouse-ui", version: "1.1.0")
expanded_diff = ConsumerProjectionRelease.diff(previous, expanded_release)

assert expanded_diff.classification == :compatible
assert %{path: "items.write.max_items", kind: :changed, classification: :compatible} in
  expanded_diff.changes
```

## 3) Stop a breaking narrowing

Removing `full_set` and lowering the collection limit invalidate requests that
were legal under the previous release.

```elixir
narrowed =
  domain
  |> put_in([:writes, :relationships, "items", :write, :modes], [:delta])
  |> put_in([:writes, :relationships, "items", :write, :max_items], 50)

assert {:ok, narrowed_release} = ConsumerProjectionRelease.compile(narrowed,
  projection_id: "order-editor", consumer: "warehouse-ui", version: "2.0.0")
narrowed_diff = ConsumerProjectionRelease.diff(previous, narrowed_release)

assert narrowed_diff.classification == :breaking
assert Enum.any?(narrowed_diff.changes, &(&1.path == "items.write.modes" and &1.kind == :narrowed))
assert Enum.any?(narrowed_diff.changes, &(&1.path == "items.write.max_items" and &1.kind == :narrowed))
```

## 4) Match the release to a certified consumer target

Declaring only half a target fails closed. A runtime/adapter pair carries its
supported feature profile into the release; that profile is evidence metadata,
not a promise about arbitrary host callbacks or databases.

```elixir
assert {:error, %{code: :incomplete_consumer_target}} =
  ConsumerProjectionRelease.compile(domain, runtime: "selecto_updato")

assert {:ok, executable_release} = ConsumerProjectionRelease.compile(domain,
  projection_id: "order-executor",
  runtime: "selecto_updato", adapter: "postgresql", feature_scope: :execution)
assert executable_release["target"]["runtime"] == "selecto_updato"
assert executable_release["feature_scope"] == "execution"
assert executable_release["required_features"] != []
```

Persist release documents beside deployed consumers and compare old-to-new in
CI. The diff is relationship-specific; ordinary field/API versioning still
needs its own compatibility policy.
Continue with [nested writes](selecto_updato_nested_writes_workbook.livemd) to
execute the order/item relationship contract.
